{"type":"message","id":"a798a086","parentId":"6256b7cc","timestamp":"2026-10-31T18:52:10.662Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Design three programs. Baseline: expose {tree threshold 1.02, consumer_tree threshold 2}, cleanup threshold 1.02.\n\nKey features from data: some decisions have fusedWork > originalWork (growth cases, e.g. example 7: 23385600 > 20364800; 501849600 > 42995584; 278208000 > 32345984; 243801600 > 30707584; 261004800 > 31526784). Those come with small producerElements (12800). The 1.02 threshold rejects them. Also example 0 has cases 1350717120→489533120 big wins with large producerElements.\n\nSmall producerElements decisions with tiny growth (example 1/3/4 tail: 16466048→16462976 etc.) — near-threshold; materialization avoided there is small anyway.\n\nCandidate variations:\n1. pointwise-fusion-sequence, same exposure as baseline but cleanup threshold 1.0 (strict, no growth) — tighter materialization avoidance? Actually baseline cleanup 1.02 accepts tiny growth. Alternative: cleanup tree on producerElements to allow growth only when producerElements large.\n2. Expose tree: accept if fusedWork<=originalWork*1.02, but for small producerElements (e.g. <=2048) be stricter (1.0) since fused loses materialization reuse? Actually exposure with growth small; questionable.\n3. Region-kind not allowed? Programs must be pointwise-fusion-sequence or fusion-sequence kinds. Yes.\n\nTree limit 31 nodes, depth 6. Keep simple.\n\nProgram A (\"candidate-a\", pointwise-fusion-sequence):\n- expose: selective, tree: if producerElements > 100000 then threshold 1.02 else threshold 1.0 (strict for tiny producers where growth decisions cluster: example 7 growth cases had producerElements 12800... hmm that's mid). Actually growth cases have producerElements 12800 or 819200 alternatives. Example 7: pairs like 31296384→11635584 (win, 819200) vs 30707584→243801600 (bad, 12800). So predicate producerElements >= 819200 allow growth (threshold 1.02→ maybe 1.1?), else 1.0. Growth >2x blocked by hard guard anyway. Let's set: if producerElements ge 262144 then threshold 1.15 else threshold 1.0. Big-win examples (0,2) have large producerElements too.\n- cleanup: threshold 1.02.\n\nProgram B (fusion-sequence, no pointwise rewrite): expose strict tree: threshold on fused ratio with consumerCount... all consumerCount=1. Use threshold 1.0 aggregate, consumer_tree threshold 1.02 (stricter than baseline 2). Cleanup: tree: if producerElements ge 262144 then accept(true)/threshold 1.05 else threshold 1.02? Cleanup threshold accepts fusion in cleanup? Cleanup policy governs materialization... Keep cleanup threshold 1.0.\n\nProgram C (pointwise-fusion-sequence): expose threshold 1.10 with tree: if originalWork ge 1e9 threshold 1.02 else... Simpler: expose threshold 1.05; cleanup tree: if producerElements le 2048 then threshold 1.0 else threshold 1.05. Distinguish from A.\n\nEnsure JSON validity. Submit three in one block? They're independent — yes.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"The traces show two clear regimes: large-producer decisions (producerElements ≥ 262144, e.g. 1.35e9→4.9e8) are dominated by large fusion wins, while small-producer decisions include many cases where fusedWork exceeds originalWork (e.g. 20.2M→23.4M, 31.5M→261M) or gains are near-zero (16466048→16462976) — these are the materialization-heavy, low-benefit cases. I'll build three programs around a producerElements split, varying exposure aggressiveness and cleanup strictness."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3a5ef68431bd4eb9aadb6503","name":"submit_policy","arguments":{"policy_json":"{\"format\":\"kiln-transformation-program/1\",\"id\":\"candidate-a-element-split\",\"kind\":\"pointwise-fusion-sequence\",\"expose\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"expose-a\",\"kind\":\"selective\",\"tree\":{\"if\":{\"feature\":\"producerElements\",\"op\":\"ge\",\"value\":262144},\"then\":{\"threshold\":1.15},\"else\":{\"threshold\":1.0}},\"consumer_tree\":{\"threshold\":1.02}},\"cleanup\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"cleanup-a\",\"kind\":\"threshold\",\"max_work_growth\":1.02}}"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a4974534552747e586ab5bc4","name":"submit_policy","arguments":{"policy_json":"{\"format\":\"kiln-transformation-program/1\",\"id\":\"candidate-b-strict-atomic\",\"kind\":\"fusion-sequence\",\"expose\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"expose-b\",\"kind\":\"selective\",\"tree\":{\"threshold\":1.0},\"consumer_tree\":{\"threshold\":1.02}},\"cleanup\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"cleanup-b\",\"kind\":\"tree\",\"tree\":{\"if\":{\"feature\":\"producerElements\",\"op\":\"ge\",\"value\":262144},\"then\":{\"threshold\":1.05},\"else\":{\"threshold\":1.0}}}}"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_80d5599cd3924e3ca46e793b","name":"submit_policy","arguments":{"policy_json":"{\"format\":\"kiln-transformation-program/1\",\"id\":\"candidate-c-wide-expose\",\"kind\":\"pointwise-fusion-sequence\",\"expose\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"expose-c\",\"kind\":\"selective\",\"tree\":{\"if\":{\"feature\":\"producerElements\",\"op\":\"le\",\"value\":2048},\"then\":{\"threshold\":1.0},\"else\":{\"threshold\":1.1}},\"consumer_tree\":{\"threshold\":1.5}},\"cleanup\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"cleanup-c\",\"kind\":\"threshold\",\"max_work_growth\":1.0}}"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"lunaroute","model":"glm-5.3-flash","usage":{"input":4505,"output":1305,"cacheRead":1024,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":766,"totalTokens":6834,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1793472712425,"responseId":"3673ec8c5a034b73b03e4b727e80cb46","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
